data: {"id":"chatcmpl_fb01fee3-94c6-4ae7-9ac3-ef249a0699fd","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792088025,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"Referee assessmentC1 is well-posed but known_false, not open. No PDF was available in the current conversation, so this assessment concerns the exact confirmed statement pasted in the request.The quantifiers are clear: for every pair of nonempty finite sets satisfying the cardinality condition, and for every function between them, there must exist two distinct domain elements with the same image. The pair may depend on the function. Neither surjectivity nor any restriction on the class of functions is assumed.The problem is the non-strict inequality, not an ambiguity in the definitions. The standard pigeonhole theorem requires  $|Y|\u003c|X|$ , as stated explicitly in the opened source’s Theorem 11.1.2. Math at Washington Equal-cardinality finite sets admit bijections, and identity functions are bijections; these are collision-free maps within C1’s permitted equality case. The opened sources therefore support a negative status for the exact assertion. Lean Community+1Separate methodological findingsCorrectness: not_checked applies to the absent submitted proof, not to the assertion’s known-status classification. There is no proof shape or live proof witness to inspect.Proof escape: undetermined for C1 as submitted. Its proposed extension beyond the standard theorem is the universal collision assertion at equality, which conflicts with the inspected baseline. An erroneous extension does not establish new mathematical content. Replacing  $\\le$  with  $\u003c$  gives the standard theorem; a proof consisting solely of instantiating that theorem and unfolding non-injectivity would be bind_only. That does not prejudge every conceivable alternative proof of the corrected statement. Math at WashingtonUtility: unsupported as a proposed general theorem because its universal guarantee is false—not because pure mathematics needs an immediate application. No information-gain model or named open-problem settlement is supplied.The appropriate feedback is to correct the inequality and identify the result as the standard pigeonhole principle, rather than display the present statement as an open question. The source records below are model-reported, not venue-verified, and the recommendation is not a publication decision.JSON{\n  \"recommendation\": \"revise\",\n  \"summary\": \"C1 is well-posed but known_false. The hypothesis permits equal-cardinality sets and bijective functions, which need not identify distinct inputs. Replacing |Y|≤|X| by |Y|\u003c|X| produces the standard pigeonhole theorem, not an open conjecture. This is feedback on the statement and its labeling, not a publication decision.\",\n  \"strengths\": [\n    \"The confirmed statement uses standard finite-set terminology and has an unambiguous universal quantifier structure.\"\n  ],\n  \"concerns\": [\n    \"The non-strict cardinality hypothesis permits collision-free bijections in the equality case.\",\n    \"The natural strict-inequality correction is already covered by the standard pigeonhole theorem and should not be presented as an open question.\"\n  ],\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"C1\",\n      \"correctness\": \"not_checked\",\n      \"shape\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": [],\n      \"note\": \"The statement is well-posed and its literature-supported status is known_false. Correctness is not_checked because no proof was submitted; this does not make the assertion's truth status unresolved. Escape is undetermined for the unchanged false assertion, with no live witnesses. Its equality-case extension is contradicted by the inspected baseline. A direct citation-and-unfolding proof of the strict correction would be bind_only, but no alternative proof has been submitted for assessment. General-theorem utility is unsupported because the proposed guarantee fails. Information gain is unassessed and settlement is absent. No attached PDF was available, so manuscript-specific conclusions are limited to the pasted C1.\",\n      \"known\": [\n        \"FINITE SETS: Cardinality \u0026 Functions between Finite Sets [opened: https://sites.math.washington.edu/~nichifor/300_2011_Spring/Chapters%2010%2011%20Finite%20Sets.pdf]\",\n        \"Lecture 16 [opened: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/2122/DiscMath/Lecture16.pdf]\",\n        \"Logic and Proof, Chapter 22: The Infinite [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/logic_and_proof/the_infinite.html]\"\n      ],\n      \"conjecture\": {\n        \"well_posed\": true,\n        \"well_posed_reason\": \"X and Y range over nonempty finite sets, |X| and |Y| denote their finite cardinalities, and h ranges over all functions X→Y. The conclusion is ∃x1,x2∈X such that x1≠x2 and h(x1)=h(x2), with the existential quantifiers inside the quantifier over h. No additional structure or map restriction is stated. The admitted equality case is a substantive mathematical defect, not a well-posedness defect.\",\n        \"status\": \"known_false\",\n        \"status_reason\": \"The condition |Y|≤|X| includes equality. The opened baselines state that equal-cardinality finite sets admit bijections and that identity functions are bijective. Such maps are injective and therefore do not satisfy the asserted collision conclusion. The standard pigeonhole theorem covers the strict case only.\",\n        \"named_works\": [\n          \"FINITE SETS: Cardinality \u0026 Functions between Finite Sets [opened: https://sites.math.washington.edu/~nichifor/300_2011_Spring/Chapters%2010%2011%20Finite%20Sets.pdf]\",\n          \"Lecture 16 [opened: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/2122/DiscMath/Lecture16.pdf]\",\n          \"Logic and Proof, Chapter 22: The Infinite [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/logic_and_proof/the_infinite.html]\"\n        ],\n        \"escape\": \"undetermined\",\n        \"escape_reason\": \"No proof was submitted, and the unchanged assertion is false, so there is no sound submitted derivation to classify as content or bind_only. The proposed extra obligation is universal non-injectivity at equality, which conflicts with the opened baseline rather than defining an unresolved advance. For the strict correction, direct theorem instantiation and definitional unfolding would be bind_only; this does not rule out content in an independently supplied alternative proof.\",\n        \"suggestions\": [\n          \"Replace |Y|≤|X| with |Y|\u003c|X| and label the corrected assertion as the standard pigeonhole theorem.\",\n          \"Explain that equal cardinalities permit bijections, so equality cannot be included while quantifying over every function.\",\n          \"Preserve the quantifier order: the two colliding inputs may depend on h.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"methodology\": {\n        \"baseline\": [\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"The proposed hypotheses are: X and Y are nonempty finite sets, |Y|≤|X|, and h:X→Y is arbitrary. No additional restriction on h is supplied.\",\n            \"location\": \"User-provided CONFIRMED STATEMENTS, entry C1, statement field; repeated verbatim in SOURCE CONTEXT.\",\n            \"source\": null\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For nonempty finite sets X,Y and a function h:X→Y, |Y|\u003c|X| implies that h is not injective.\",\n            \"location\": \"PDF page 1, Theorem 11.1.2.\",\n            \"source\": \"FINITE SETS: Cardinality \u0026 Functions between Finite Sets [opened: https://sites.math.washington.edu/~nichifor/300_2011_Spring/Chapters%2010%2011%20Finite%20Sets.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For every set A, the identity function id_A:A→A is bijective; its defining rule is id_A(a)=a.\",\n            \"location\": \"PDF page 17, printed page 376, Theorem 130; identity-function rule on PDF page 7, printed page 372, NB 2.\",\n            \"source\": \"Lecture 16 [opened: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/2122/DiscMath/Lecture16.pdf]\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"statement\": \"For finite sets A and B, equality of cardinalities is equivalent to existence of a bijection between A and B.\",\n            \"location\": \"Section 22.1, Equinumerosity, opening paragraph.\",\n            \"source\": \"Logic and Proof, Chapter 22: The Infinite [opened: https://leanprover-community.github.io/logic_and_proof/the_infinite.html]\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"searched\": [\n          \"Current-conversation Files s"},"finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl_fb01fee3-94c6-4ae7-9ac3-ef249a0699fd","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1792088025,"model":"oracle/chrono-chatgpt-pro-pool","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"earch using the supplied title and complete C1 assertion returned no files present. No PDF was available to inspect.\",\n          \"Web search query: site.edu pigeonhole principle function finite sets |A| \u003e |B| injective identity\",\n          \"Web search query: site.edu finite sets same cardinality bijection pigeonhole principle\",\n          \"Web search query: site.edu \\\"identity function\\\" \\\"bijection\\\" \\\"finite\\\"\",\n          \"Web search query: site.edu \\\"same cardinality\\\" \\\"if and only if\\\" \\\"bijection\\\" finite sets\",\n          \"Inspected the three opened sources recorded in baseline. Visually checked the page containing Theorem 11.1.2 and the page containing Theorem 130. The scope was the elementary strict pigeonhole theorem and its equality boundary.\",\n          \"One additional search-result webpage failed to open because of a timeout. It remains not_checkable and was excluded from the baseline. No conclusion depends on that retrieval.\"\n        ],\n        \"literature\": \"supported\",\n        \"binding_analysis\": \"Unfolding the collision conclusion gives ∃x1,x2∈X, x1≠x2 and h(x1)=h(x2), the classical negation of injectivity. Renaming variables and instantiating the strict pigeonhole theorem yields this conclusion when |Y|\u003c|X|. Under the submitted premises, assumption substitution or normalization cannot replace ≤ by \u003c. Projecting injectivity from bijectivity instead identifies the equality-case obstruction. Recombining the strict and equality cases does not supply C1. No manuscript aliases, wrappers, helper definitions, tactic expansions or proof steps were provided to inspect.\",\n        \"delta\": \"The exact additional assertion beyond the strict baseline would be: for every pair of nonempty finite sets X,Y with |X|=|Y|, every function h:X→Y has two distinct inputs with the same value. This is the conditional obligation introduced by the weak inequality. It conflicts with the inspected bijection baseline and is not an unresolved research gap. Removing equality removes this extra obligation and leaves the known theorem.\",\n        \"escape\": \"undetermined\",\n        \"witnesses\": [],\n        \"utility\": {\n          \"kind\": \"general_theorem\",\n          \"status\": \"unsupported\",\n          \"rationale\": \"A general theorem needs no artificial downstream application. Here the proposed mathematical guarantee itself fails, so it cannot establish general-theorem utility. The referee's status correction is not a new result supplied by the submission.\",\n          \"problem\": \"Do nonempty finite X,Y with |Y|≤|X| force every function h:X→Y to identify two distinct inputs?\",\n          \"progress\": \"The opened baseline supports a negative answer to the exact assertion and identifies the strict-inequality correction as already known.\",\n          \"prerequisites\": \"Finiteness and nonemptiness of X,Y; |Y|≤|X|; h an arbitrary function X→Y, with no exclusion of bijections.\",\n          \"discharge\": \"The equality case is expressly permitted, and the opened baseline supplies bijections in that case. Consequently the proposed universal conclusion is not discharged. No proof of C1 was submitted.\"\n        },\n        \"settlement\": null,\n        \"information_gain\": null,\n        \"limits\": [\n          \"The source comparison is supported only within the stated elementary finite-set scope; no exhaustive historical, priority or novelty search was performed.\",\n          \"No attached PDF was available. Local locations refer only to the supplied C1 statement and SOURCE CONTEXT.\",\n          \"Correctness not_checked concerns an absent proof. The known_false status is a separate mathematical finding.\",\n          \"Escape undetermined does not suggest that C1 remains open or might have established value; there is no admissible submitted proof to inspect.\",\n          \"The strict correction has a bind-only derivation from the baseline, but no claim is made that every possible proof of that known theorem must be bind_only.\",\n          \"Source records are model-reported and have not undergone independent venue verification.\",\n          \"No compiled registration, Lean verification, computational certificate, AURIC/FIB measurement or information-gain score is claimed.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"limits\": [\n    \"This report evaluates the exact pasted assertion, not an unavailable PDF or a hypothetical corrected manuscript.\",\n    \"The negative truth-status finding is distinct from the absence of a submitted proof, which leaves proof correctness not_checked and proof escape undetermined.\",\n    \"Utility is unsupported because the stated guarantee fails, not because an immediate application is missing. No information-gain analysis or open-problem settlement is claimed.\",\n    \"Only a bounded search and the recorded opened sources support the comparison. An unsuccessful additional retrieval was not_checkable and was not used.\",\n    \"The structured methodology is this referee's account, not an independent source-auditor certification. No recommendation determines publication.\"\n  ],\n  \"text\": \"Revise the cardinality hypothesis and display labeling. As written, C1 is a well-posed false assertion because equality allows bijections. With strict inequality, it is the standard pigeonhole theorem. No proof, proof-escape witness, independent new utility or named open-problem settlement is established by this statement-only submission.\"\n}"},"finish_reason":null}]}

